[{:db/id 17592186332144 :question/problem "Find the constant particular solution to the following Clairaut differential equation:\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{′}+{y}^{′}-3.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A constant particular solution has $y'=0$. Substituting this into\n\n$$y=xy'+y'-3$$\n\ngives $y=-3$. Its derivative is zero, and direct substitution verifies\n\n$$-3=x\\cdot0+0-3.$$\n\nTherefore, the constant particular solution is $y=-3$.\n\nTo distinguish this from a singular solution, write $p=y'$. The equation becomes $y=(x+1)p-3$, and differentiation gives $0=(x+1)p'$. The ordinary straight-line family is\n\n$$y=c(x+1)-3,$$\n\nwith arbitrary constant $c$. The envelope condition obtained by differentiating this family with respect to $c$ is $x+1=0$. It forces $x=-1$ and $y=-3$, giving a single point rather than a singular graph $y(x)$. The constant solution $y=-3$ is the ordinary family member $c=0$; it is not a separate singular solution." :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
